- title "GitLab"

%h1= title

%h2 Signing in and connecting
%p
  Sign in with GitLab from the sign-in page, or, if you already sign in with GitHub, connect GitLab from
  your settings page. Either way you are one person on #{hostname} with an account on each forge.
%p
  Your top-level GitLab groups become organisations here, the way GitHub organisations do. Projects in
  subgroups cannot be added yet.

%h2 How morph.io reaches your repositories
%p
  On GitHub, morph.io reaches repositories through an App you install. GitLab has no equivalent that every
  plan offers, so morph.io uses the most durable credential your plan and your role allow, and falls through
  to weaker ones. The "GitLab access" panel on an organisation's page shows which is in force.
%dl
  %dt Group access token
  %dd
    A token morph.io holds itself, as a Reporter-level member of the group. Scrapers keep running when people
    leave. Needs the group to be on GitLab Premium or Ultimate, and someone with Owner on the group to click
    "Connect GitLab group".
  %dt Group deploy token plus your sign-in
  %dd
    Available on every plan. The deploy token lets morph.io clone the code durably; who may see and run each
    scraper is refreshed through the GitLab sign-in of any collaborator. If everyone who signed in with GitLab
    leaves the group, runs go ahead on the permissions last seen and the scraper shows a warning until someone
    signs in again.
  %dt Your sign-in alone
  %dd
    What a scraper under your own account uses. If you disconnect GitLab, or your token stops working, its
    runs fail with a message saying so.

%h2 What is different from GitHub
%ul
  %li
    Contributors are not listed for GitLab scrapers. GitLab's API reports the people in a repository's history by
    name and email rather than username, and morph.io does not look people up by email.
  %li
    New repositories created from #{hostname} do not link back to their scraper page, because GitLab projects have
    no homepage field.
  %li
    Roles map onto GitHub's permissions: Reporter and above can see a private scraper; Developer and above can
    change and run it; Maintainer and Owner can change its settings and delete it.

%h2#moving Moving a scraper between forges
%p
  A scraper's admin can point it at a different repository from the Danger Zone on its settings page: on
  another forge or the same one. The scraper keeps its address on #{hostname}, its data, its history of runs
  and its watchers. The repository has to belong to the scraper's owner on the new forge, so a scraper cannot
  quietly change hands. The old clone is discarded and the code fetched afresh.
